rd-break: break-word; caret-color: transparent; padding: 3px 2px;">Initiall=
y, (OpenAI) people believed that the performance of language models w.r.t. =
model size can be predicted by a log-linear curve, where the <span style=3D=
"font-weight:600" data-token-index=3D"1" class=3D"notion-enable-hover">perf=
ormance increase linearly as the model size increase exponentially</span>. =
This behavior is called <span style=3D"font-weight:600" data-token-index=3D=
"3" class=3D"notion-enable-hover">the scaling law</span> of language models=
, as is initially discussed in Kaplan et. al. 2020 then observed in GPT-3 O=
riginal paper. Importantly, at this stage, <span style=3D"font-weight:600" =
data-token-index=3D"5" class=3D"notion-enable-hover">prompting even the lar=
gest GPT-3 cannot outperform fine-tuning smaller models</span>. This cast t=
he question of why bother using so large expensive models (even prompting i=
s annotation-efficient). Later in Cobbe et. al. 2021, the scaling law seems=
 also to apply to fine-tuning. This is rather pessimistic because it implie=
s that <span style=3D"font-weight:600" data-token-index=3D"7" class=3D"noti=
on-enable-hover">we are maybe locked to our model scale</span> =E2=80=94 di=
fferent tweaks of model architectures may improve the model performance to =
a certain extent, but still, <span style=3D"font-weight:600" data-token-ind=
ex=3D"9" class=3D"notion-enable-hover">we seem to be locked in its correspo=
nding scale range and cannot have a significant performance breakthrough</s=
pan>. </div></div></div></div><div data-block-id=3D"bee24cf5-71ae-419a-8b11=
